Do java spring boot use http in code and https at gateway?

Yes. **Spring Boot can use exactly the same architecture.**

The common production setup is:

```text
Client
   │
   │ HTTPS :443
   ▼
Nginx / API Gateway
   │
   │ HTTP :8080
   ▼
Spring Boot REST API
```

### In Spring Boot code

You simply write a normal HTTP REST API:

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api")
public class HelloController {

    @GetMapping("/hello")
    public String hello() {
        return "Hello from Spring Boot";
    }
}
```

Spring Boot runs on something like:

```text
http://localhost:8080/api/hello
```

Nginx handles HTTPS:

```text
https://example.com/api/hello
        │
        ▼
      Nginx
        │
        ▼
http://localhost:8080/api/hello
```

This is often called **TLS/SSL termination at the reverse proxy**.

### But Spring Boot can also do HTTPS itself

Just like POCO:

```text
Client
   │ HTTPS
   ▼
Spring Boot :8443
```

You configure an SSL certificate/keystore in `application.properties`, for example:

```properties
server.port=8443
server.ssl.enabled=true
server.ssl.key-store=classpath:server.p12
server.ssl.key-store-password=password
server.ssl.key-store-type=PKCS12
```

So the important distinction is:

| Setup                    | API code | TLS         |
| ------------------------ | -------- | ----------- |
| Nginx + Spring Boot      | HTTP     | Nginx       |
| Nginx + POCO             | HTTP     | Nginx       |
| Direct Spring Boot HTTPS | HTTP API | Spring Boot |
| Direct POCO HTTPS        | HTTP API | POCO        |

For the **POCO + Nginx architecture you're learning**,
the equivalent **Spring Boot + Nginx architecture is almost identical**.
